config FIBRIL_CAN_NODE_ODRIVE
	bool
	default y
	depends on DT_HAS_FIBRIL_FCAN_ODRIVE_ENABLED
	select MOTOR
	select MOTOR_ODRIVE
	select ODRIVE_SEQUENCER
	help
	  Compiled whenever the devicetree describes the axes, which is also
	  what puts ODriveMotor instances into the schema. Describing the
	  wiring is the whole of adding this function to an image.

config FIBRIL_CAN_NODE_ODRIVE_MAX
	int "ODriveMotor instances to allocate for"
	default 8
	range 1 32
	depends on FIBRIL_CAN_NODE_ODRIVE
	help
	  The schema's max_count for ODriveMotor: how many instances the
	  generated code reserves state for. It is a ceiling, not a count; the
	  bus and the ROS graph see only the axes the devicetree names.

	  The driver has a ceiling of its own, MOTOR_ODRIVE_MAX_AXES, which
	  counts the axes on each odrive,bus node. The axes this function names
	  may sit on more than one bus, so neither ceiling derives from the
	  other. Overshooting the driver's makes its registration fail at init,
	  which this function reports as a failed device_is_ready().
